> What's the issue in this one? I'm not seeing it.

I should have given a more detailed explanation. I'm sorry.

It looks like the both `LastLocationInput` and `LastIndirectLocationInput` properties may return `null`. 

```csharp
internal SectionInput LastLocationInput => HasLocationInputs ? LocationInputs[LocationInputs.Count - 1] : null;
```
[Link to the sources](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/d099f075e45d2aa6007a22b71b45a08758559f80/src/libraries/System.Configuration.ConfigurationManager/src/System/Configuration/SectionRecord.cs#L110)

```csharp
internal SectionInput LastIndirectLocationInput
  => HasIndirectLocationInputs ? IndirectLocationInputs[IndirectLocationInputs.Count - 1] : null;
```

[Link to the sources](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/d099f075e45d2aa6007a22b71b45a08758559f80/src/libraries/System.Configuration.ConfigurationManager/src/System/Configuration/SectionRecord.cs#L121)

The value of `LastIndirectLocationInput` is assigned to the `input` variable. After the assignment, `input` is checked for `null`. After this the `HasResult` property is also checked:
```csharp
SectionInput input = sectionRecord.LastIndirectLocationInput;
Debug.Assert(input != null);
Debug.Assert(input.HasResult, "input.HasResult");
```

The value of the `LastLocalInput` is assigned to the `input` variable. `input` is also checked for the result (`HasResult`), but 
 is not checked for the `null` value:
```csharp
SectionInput input = sectionRecord.LastLocationInput;
// Debug.Assert(input != null) ?
Debug.Assert(input.HasResult, "input.HasResult");
```

It looks a little bit suspicious.
